-- host.lua -- machine-specific Hyprland settings.
--
-- Copy to ~/.config/hypr/host.lua and edit. NOT tracked by this repo and NOT
-- linked by install.sh: it is the one file that is meant to differ per machine,
-- which is exactly what should not be shared between them.
--
-- hyprland.lua loads it with pcall(dofile, ...) before anything else, so it can
-- set globals the rest of the config reads -- WSBANDS in particular.
--
-- Hyprland's autoreload watches the config files it parses itself. This one is
-- reached through dofile and is not among them, so a change here lands with
-- `hyprctl reload` (or the next login) and not on save.
--
-- Find your monitor names and ids with:  hyprctl monitors

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Monitors
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- hl.monitor({ output = "DP-3",     mode = "preferred", position = "auto", scale = "auto" })
-- hl.monitor({ output = "HDMI-A-1", mode = "preferred", position = "auto", scale = "auto",
--              transform = 1 })  -- 1 = 90 degrees, i.e. portrait

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Lid, on a laptop
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- logind suspends on a lid close only while nothing else is attached: a second
-- connected display counts as docked, and HandleLidSwitchDocked defaults to
-- ignore. So on a docked laptop the switch reaches nothing, and the built-in
-- panel stays lit -- windows and all -- under a shut lid. Blank it instead.
--
-- lib/lid.lua is the mechanism, and its header is the reasoning. Two lines of
-- it worth knowing before you enable this:
--
--   * the panel is off only while the lid is shut AND something else is
--     actually driving a screen, so losing the external display -- to a resume,
--     a pulled cable, or the monitor's own power saving -- brings the panel
--     back on its own. The alternative is a compositor with no outputs, which
--     is a session you can only reboot out of. See decision 023.
--   * `MOD + CTRL + D` forces the panel back on regardless, and works with the
--     lock screen up. The escape hatch, if the rule above ever misses a case.
--
-- All this file supplies is which output the panel is, and where it sits. Give
-- it an explicit position rather than "auto": with auto, an output that comes
-- back after being disabled is placed after the one that stayed, so opening the
-- lid used to put the laptop panel to the RIGHT of the ultrawide -- measured
-- 2026-08-26.
--
-- And give the panel NO hl.monitor() line of its own above: lid.setup() is its
-- monitor rule. A second one lights the panel at every reload for the lid
-- module to put out again, and an output that comes and goes that fast takes
-- every client down with it -- decision 023, "Off waits; on never does".

-- With the lid module in use, declare the EXTERNAL monitors through it too --
-- lid.external() in place of hl.monitor() above, same spec, `desc:` rules
-- included. They are then disabled across sleep and given back once the link
-- has settled, so a DP link retraining on resume has no wl_output to churn.
-- bin/hypr-sleepwatch is the trigger, and setup() starts it.
--
-- local lid = require("lib.lid")   -- at the top of the file
-- lid.external({ output = "DP-3", mode = "preferred", position = "1920x0", scale = "1" })
--
-- lid.setup({ output = "eDP-1", mode = "preferred", position = "0x0", scale = "1" })

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rotation, on a convertible
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- The panel turns as the device turns, once the accelerometer's reading has
-- held for a second; `MOD + CTRL + R` locks it where it is. Open as a laptop the
-- panel reads upright, so in practice it only turns once folded.
--
-- lib/rotate.lua is the mechanism, and its header says why it is not limited to
-- tablet mode. It needs iio-sensor-proxy, which the system group of the package
-- manifest installs.
--
-- Hand it the SAME spec as lid.setup(), as one table: with the lid module in use
-- the rotation goes through it, so its re-applies keep the panel turned.
--
-- local rotate = require("lib.rotate")   -- at the top of the file
-- local PANEL  = { output = "eDP-1", mode = "preferred", position = "0x0", scale = "1" }
-- lid.setup(PANEL)
-- rotate.setup(PANEL)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Workspace bands
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- One entry per monitor. `base` must be the monitor's id x WORKSPACE_BAND (10),
-- so monitor 0 owns workspaces 1-10, monitor 1 owns 11-20, and so on.
--
-- `direction` is the scrolling layout's own axis for that band -- which is the
-- point of doing it per monitor. A portrait screen is far more usable scrolling
-- down than sideways.
--
-- `column_width` is the default fraction of the monitor a new column takes.
-- A portrait monitor wants 1.0 (one column fills the width); a wide landscape
-- one wants a third. Hyprland has no per-monitor form of this option, so
-- lib/colwidth.lua applies it as focus moves between monitors -- which is why
-- this is a real per-band setting and not decoration. A band that omits it gets
-- COLUMN_WIDTH from conf/options.lua.

-- WSBANDS = {
--     { base =  0, monitor = "DP-3",     direction = "right", column_width = 0.333 },
--     { base = 10, monitor = "HDMI-A-1", direction = "down",  column_width = 1.0   },
-- }

